+# Retrieval scenarios
+
+Status: synthetic reasoning cases under the [provisional model](model.md).
+Date: 2026-10-02. Revision: 1.
+
+All identifiers and names here are fabricated. Expected answers exercise assumptions, not SQL or runtime behavior.
+
+## Ordinary case: shared participation and coverage
+
+Inputs: organisation O-1 “Example Amber” and organisation O-2 “Example Birch” both participate in agreement A-1. O-1 also participates in A-2. A-1 and A-2 each cover service S-1 “Example inspection”. Organisation O-3 “Example Cedar” has no agreements. Service S-2 “Example cataloguing” has no agreements.
+
+- Given O-1, retrieve A-1 and A-2 exactly once each. Given O-2, retrieve A-1. Path: organisation → participation → agreement. This exercises A1 and A3.
+- For A-1 and A-2, retrieve S-1 for each. Path: agreement → coverage → service. This exercises A2, including one service shared by agreements.
+- Given O-3, retrieve no agreements. S-2 appears in no agreement's coverage result. These exercise the optional ends of A1–A2.
+
+## Identity counterexample
+
+Inputs: rename O-1 to “Example Birch”, retaining its identifier and participation. O-2 still has that same label and its own identifier.
+
+Expected: the results for O-1 and O-2 remain as above. Name-only lookup is ambiguous and must not merge them. Path: identified organisation → participation → agreement. This exercises A4; name search and disambiguation behavior remain outside scope.
+
+## Missing participant counterexample
+
+Input: proposed A-3 covers S-1 but has no organisation.
+
+Expected under A1: A-3 cannot be represented as an admissible agreement. Whether the domain must allow this case is unresolved. The organisation → participation → agreement path cannot discover it from any organisation. This case challenges the agreement's minimum participation, rather than proving empty agreements are forbidden in the actual domain.
+
+## Several services counterexample
+
+Input: proposed A-4 involves O-1 and covers both S-1 and S-2.
+
+Expected under A2: this case violates the single-service assumption; there is no justified choice of just one result. If the domain permits it, agreement → coverage → service must return both, and the canonical model must change. Real-domain acceptance remains unresolved.
+
+## Multiple participant roles counterexample
+
+Input: O-1 participates in A-1 in two proposed, unnamed capacities.
+
+Expected for the supplied retrieval question: A-1 still appears once for O-1 under A3. Whether the capacities are valid or need separate relationships cannot be answered until roles are defined. Path: organisation → participation → distinct agreement. Role-specific retrieval is not supported by this proposal.
